{
  "requirement": "Habeas uses the TBV [outline selector grammar](sdk/outlines.md#parse-an-outline-selector)\nunder {cite}`requirements.sdk.outlines.selector_syntax` for source addresses.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligation.** `requirements.references.outline_selectors` has one obligation. Habeas source addresses must use the TBV outline selector grammar defined by `requirements.sdk.outlines.selector_syntax`. That cited requirement is context, so I did not reassess its own tests. I only checked that the tests show addresses following the grammar.\n\n**Mapping to tests.**\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/search.rs#fn[outline_addresses_validate_selectors_without_reading_paths]` exercises address construction through `OutlineData::new`.\n  - It accepts the empty selector (root), `fn[kept]`, and `mod[a%2Fb]/fn[kept]` (a percent-encoded `/` in a name). It asserts that the path and selector are kept unchanged.\n  - It rejects `fn[missing` (unterminated bracket), `fn[bad%ZZ]` (malformed percent escape) and `/fn[kept]` (leading `/`).\n  - So address construction applies grammar validation in both the accept and reject directions.\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/src/outline/tests.rs#fn[rust_selectors_follow_syntactic_ancestry]` checks that generated selectors resolve and round-trip to themselves. It covers:\n  - `/`-separated paths;\n  - `#N` disambiguation, where `fn[repeated]` without `#N` fails to resolve;\n  - percent-encoding, as in `impl[%5Bu8;%204%5D]`;\n  - the empty selector resolving to root.\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/src/outline/tests.rs#fn[anonymous_selectors_use_identity_hashes]` checks the `kind@` plus 12-hex-digit form and `#1`/`#2` for identical siblings.\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/src/outline/tests.rs#fn[anonymous_container_selectors_use_identity_hashes_and_survive_descendant_edits]` checks that selectors for anonymous containers use the identity hash.\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/src/outline/tests.rs#fn[plain_text_lines_are_interchangeable_anonymous_nodes]` checks that identical `line` selectors resolve to any matching line.\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/src/outline/tests.rs#fn[profile_rules_map_only_matching_contexts_and_keep_other_nodes_transparent]` checks a missing selector and a malformed escape. The malformed escape returns `ResolveError::Invalid` with a message that names the problem.\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/src/outline/markdown_tests.rs#fn[paragraphs_and_nested_directives_have_independent_source_ranges]` checks that Markdown selectors resolve and that anonymous segments take the `kind@<12 hex>` form.\n\n**Gaps.** I found none. All of these tests carry `{test}` references to the subject. The address-level test checks the Habeas-facing behavior: addresses use the grammar and reject malformed selectors. The grammar details are covered by the outline tests across several source kinds. The address test does not feed `kind@hash` or `#N` selectors through `OutlineData::new`. That is the same kind of grammar-valid input it already accepts, and those forms are tested at the grammar level, so I do not count it as a gap. No acknowledgments (`{fixme}`) apply.",
  "references": "2c6fb45ec8a367d6739dd65005439f9370f78d69a364f7f23056d35dcb5f7f73",
